# From Composition to the Higher-Order Boundary

Finite EML expressions combine constants, variables, addition, multiplication, exponential, and logarithm. Begin by changing a tower's depth and watching structural size grow.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

The underlying law is simple: the tower $E_0=0$ and $E_{k+1}=\exp(E_k)$ has size $k+1$.

{{visualization:1}}

## Composition works exactly

Simultaneous substitution is symbolic composition, and evaluation after substitution equals ordinary functional composition. Explore the complete executable example, then inspect the substitution algorithm.

{{demo:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the substitution theorem and proof sketch</summary>
For an expression $e$ in $m$ variables and replacements $\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m$ in $n$ variables,
$$\llbracket e[\sigma]\rrbracket(x)=\llbracket e\rrbracket(\llbracket\sigma_1\rrbracket(x),\ldots,\llbracket\sigma_m\rrbracket(x)).$$
Structural induction handles constants and variables directly and propagates the equality through each unary or binary constructor. Variable substitution is the identity, and nested substitutions associate; hence finite EML programs obey both identity laws and associativity.
</details>

## Pairing makes multi-output programs

Concatenating output tuples defines $\langle f,g\rangle$, and
$$\langle f,g\rangle\circ h=\langle f\circ h,g\circ h\rangle.$$
The numerical demonstration evaluates this equation together with the category laws.

{{demo:1}}

For general background, see the [nLab discussion of Cartesian categories](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cartesian+category) and the [Stanford Encyclopedia introduction to category theory](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/category-theory/).

## Why parameter sharing stops short

Leaf parameters alter labels but preserve tree size. Try specializing a six-node template yourself.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the size-invariance proof</summary>
A leaf substitution replaces every parameter by a one-node constant or variable. Induction on the template shows that leaves retain size $1$, binary nodes retain $1+|a|+|b|$, and unary nodes retain $1+|a|$. Therefore $|T[\sigma]|=|T|$.
</details>

## Build the escaping witness

For a proposed template of size $s$, choose $E_s$. It has size $s+1$, while every leaf specialization has size $s$.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the complete obstruction argument</summary>
Assume a finite template $T$ is universal by leaf parameter sharing, and set $s=|T|$. Universality would produce a leaf substitution with $T[\sigma]=E_s$. Size invariance gives $|T[\sigma]|=s$, while the tower theorem gives $|E_s|=s+1$. Equality of trees would imply $s=s+1$, a contradiction. Thus no fixed finite template is universal by this mechanism.
</details>

## What follows—and what remains open

The result blocks a specific parameter-sharing construction of categorical exponentials. It does not rule out extensional categories that identify expressions computing the same function, nor richer representations containing codes, closures, or interpreters. A full finite-product treatment should add projections and beta/eta laws; a Cartesian-closed treatment must exhibit actual function-space objects and the currying bijection.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{demo:2}}

Continue with the [nLab overview of Cartesian closed categories](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category) to compare the required universal property with the finite-template mechanism.

